+## Conversation List
+
+A conversation list tells a client what else the authenticated user has. With `transport: {history: {enabled: true}}`, a client can request the list with `list_conversations` and receive `conversation_list` carrying a `ConversationSummary[]` (see [websocket-protocol.md](../specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md)). A summary is `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount`, with its dates as ISO strings.
+
+<!-- doc-block: none -- illustrates the frame exchange -->
+
+```typescript
+ws.send(JSON.stringify({type: 'list_conversations', limit: 20}));
+
+ws.onmessage = event => {
+  const message = JSON.parse(event.data);
+  if (message.type === 'conversation_list') {
+    console.log('Conversations:', message.conversations);
+  }
+};
+```
+
+**Opt-in.** History is off by default. A deployment that turns it on should also turn on `resume`, so a user can actually open the conversations the list reveals; without resume, the engine warns at startup and the client receives a list whose rows it cannot rejoin.
+
+**Store compatibility.** The engine queries the store's optional `listFor(userId, options)` to build the list. A store that implements no `listFor` answers `list_conversations` with an `error` frame carrying `UNSUPPORTED`, as it does when the feature is disabled.
+
+**GDPR.** A conversation list is personal data. It carries no conversation content, it is taken from the connection's authenticated user and never from the frame, and it respects the same retention bounds as resume. See re-cinq/HALEngine#41.
+
 ## Session Resume
 
 By default every connection gets a new, empty session. With `transport: {resume: {enabled: true}}`, a client that reconnects can get its conversation back: it opens `{basePath}/ws?sessionId=<id>` with the `sessionId` from its last `connected` frame. The server rejoins that conversation only if it belongs to the connection's authenticated user, answers `resumed: true` with an `entryCount`, and replays each stored entry as an `entry_upsert` at its index, or as an `entry_skip` for one a tool suppressed. Any other id, whether another user's, one never issued or one the store no longer holds, gets a fresh session and `resumed: false`, and the three are indistinguishable. The id is not a credential: it is checked against the user your `WsAuthenticator` returned.
